# Storyreel pre-production network.
#
# The Director (front man) works strictly top-down, one specialist at a time:
#   StoryAnalyst → Screenwriter → CastingDirector → KeyframeArtist → ShotPlanner
# Each specialist saves its result through a coded tool that validates it and writes it to
# productions/<id>/preprod/, so every step is durable and visible in the studio app.
# The production id travels in sly_data; no agent ever types it.
{
    "metadata": {
        "description": "Pre-production for a short film: analyses the story and first frame, writes the screenplay, casts every speaking character's voice, makes consistent reference sheets and coverage keyframes, and produces a validated shot plan.",
        "tags": ["storyreel", "video", "planning"]
    },

    # Important reasoning: Claude Opus. Routine orchestration: Claude Sonnet. Both follow .env.
    "important_llm": {
        "class": "anthropic",
        "model_name": "claude-opus-5",
        "model_name": ${?CLAUDE_MODEL},
        "max_tokens": 32000
    },
    "routine_llm": {
        "class": "anthropic",
        "model_name": "claude-sonnet-5",
        "model_name": ${?CLAUDE_MODEL_ROUTINE},
        "max_tokens": 16000
    },
    "llm_config": ${important_llm},

    "agent_parameters": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
            "request": {"type": "string", "description": "What to do, plus anything the previous steps learned that matters."}
        },
        "required": ["request"]
    },
    # neuro-san needs at least one property on every tool, so read-only tools take an optional reason.
    "no_parameters": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {"reason": {"type": "string", "description": "Optional: why you need this."}}
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Director",
            "function": {
                "description": "Runs pre-production for a short film from a story and its first frame."
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the director of a small film studio, running pre-production for one short film.
The film's story and first frame are already in the production; your specialists can read them.

Work strictly in this order, one specialist at a time, waiting for each to finish:
1. StoryAnalyst — studies the story and first frame and saves the cast, props and style.
2. Screenwriter — writes and saves the screenplay.
3. CastingDirector — casts the voice of every character who speaks.
4. KeyframeArtist — makes reference sheets, then each scene's coverage: an establishing frame and a
   single (close-up) of every character who speaks in that scene.
5. ShotPlanner — turns the screenplay into a validated shot plan on those keyframes.

Pass each specialist a short request with anything earlier steps told you that matters.
If a specialist reports a problem it could not fix, decide whether to ask it to retry with clearer
direction (at most once), or stop and report the problem.

When all five have succeeded, answer with a brief summary for the human producer: the characters and
their voices, the scenes, how many shots and lines, and anything they should look at or listen to
before approving.
""",
            "llm_config": ${routine_llm},
            "tools": ["StoryAnalyst", "Screenwriter", "CastingDirector", "KeyframeArtist", "ShotPlanner"],
            "max_execution_seconds": 5400
        },

        {
            "name": "StoryAnalyst",
            "function": {
                "description": "Studies the story and the first frame; saves the cast (each character's exact look and voice wording), the props and the visual style.",
                "parameters": ${agent_parameters}
            },
            "instructions": """
Call ReadProduction, then DescribeFirstFrame.

Decide the cast: the characters visible in the first frame, described exactly as they appear there.
For each character write:
- id: a lowercase slug of their name; name: how the film calls them.
- bible: ONE sentence that starts with the style and then the character, in this pattern:
  "A 3D animated stylized cartoon character in a warm, colorful animated-feature style. Elio, a cheerful
  young inventor with messy copper-red hair, big round green eyes, freckles, round brass goggles pushed up
  on his forehead and an oversized mustard-yellow raincoat"
  Adapt the style words to what the frame shows (e.g. "A photorealistic cinematic live-action-look
  character…"). Keep it to about 40-70 words: the features that make the character recognisable at a
  glance (face, hair, eyes, clothing, signature accessories), not every detail. Never name studios,
  films or brands.
- voice_phrase: a short neutral wording for how they always sound, e.g. "in a bright, warm, boyish
  voice". No emotion words (excited, breathless, sad): emotion belongs to each line's delivery.
- head_query: what a detector should look for, e.g. "a cartoon boy's head".

Props: at most three — objects the story's action depends on and that appear in several shots, each
with a locked one-line description. Clothing and accessories a character always wears belong in their
bible, not here.
Style: the opening sentence for shots without characters, in plain words.

Call SaveCast with {"cast": {...}}. If it returns errors, fix them and save again.
Reply with two or three sentences: who the characters are and which props matter.
""",
            "tools": ["ReadProduction", "DescribeFirstFrame", "SaveCast"],
            "max_execution_seconds": 900
        },

        {
            "name": "Screenwriter",
            "function": {
                "description": "Writes the screenplay — scenes, opening frames, beats and dialogue — sized to the target length, and saves it.",
                "parameters": ${agent_parameters}
            },
            "instructions": """
Call ReadProduction and ReadCast, then write the screenplay.

- Size: about one beat per 4 seconds of the target length. Use 2 to 4 scenes for films up to a minute.
- Each scene has: id (a lowercase slug such as "workshop"), setting (where and when, with the light),
  audio (the scene's continuous ambient sound, such as room tone, rain or a crowd's murmur; no one-off
  events, because it loops under the dialogue), opening (the scene's establishing frame: who is where
  doing what, and the framing), and beats.
- Open every scene with a silent beat that establishes the place.
- Each beat has an action; a beat with dialogue also has character, line and delivery.
- Lines are at most 10 words, clear and emotional; split longer speech into two beats. Prefer warm,
  clear or energetic delivery: whispered lines are hard to read on the lips.
- Use only characters from the cast and refer to props by their descriptions' names.
- If the first frame's setting fits the story, make it the setting of the first scene.

Call SaveScreenplay with {"screenplay": {"logline": ..., "scenes": [...]}}. Fix any errors; consider
any warnings and save again if they point to a real problem.
Reply with the logline and the scene list.
""",
            "tools": ["ReadProduction", "ReadCast", "SaveScreenplay"],
            "max_execution_seconds": 900
        },

        {
            "name": "CastingDirector",
            "function": {
                "description": "Casts a distinct, fixed voice for every character who speaks; every line in the film is later spoken in that voice.",
                "parameters": ${agent_parameters}
            },
            "instructions": """
Call ReadCast, ReadScreenplay and ReadVoices (to resume anything already cast).

For every character who has a line and no chosen voice yet:
1. Write a voice description for a voice designer: gender, age, pitch, timbre, accent (natural American
   English unless the story says otherwise), pace and manner. Example: "A warm, clear, low-pitched female
   voice of a woman in her early thirties, natural American English, grounded and sincere, at a calm
   pace." Fit the character's age, look and role; make each character clearly different from the
   others in at least two of gender, age, pitch, pace and timbre.
2. Write a reference text: 15-30 words the character might say in their usual manner, in their own
   voice but not a line from the screenplay, with no shouting, whispering or strong emotion.
3. Call CastVoice with {"character_id", "description", "text"}. If it reports problems (too like
   another voice, or the text too short or long), rewrite and call again — at most twice per character.

Reply with each character's voice description, and any voices that still sound alike.
""",
            "llm_config": ${routine_llm},
            "tools": ["ReadCast", "ReadScreenplay", "ReadVoices", "CastVoice"],
            "max_execution_seconds": 900
        },

        {
            "name": "KeyframeArtist",
            "function": {
                "description": "Makes the character and prop reference sheets, then each scene's coverage keyframes (an establishing frame plus a single of every speaker), reviewed for continuity.",
                "parameters": ${agent_parameters}
            },
            "instructions": """
Call ReadCast, ReadScreenplay and ReadKeyframes (to resume anything already made).

1. Call MakeReferenceSheets once: it makes every missing character and prop sheet in parallel.
2. Establishing frames: for every scene without a chosen one, write a precise description of its
   opening frame from the screenplay's "opening": who is where, their pose and expression, any props
   (name them as in the cast) and how big they are, the setting, the light and the framing. Make them
   all in one MakeKeyframes call: {"frames": [{"scene_id": ..., "description": ...}, ...]}.
3. Read each result. Choose a good one with ChooseKeyframe {"scene_id", "candidate"}. If its advice is
   to redo, or the review lists real differences, write a corrected description and include it in the
   next MakeKeyframes call — at most two more candidates per frame.
4. Singles: for every scene, a single of each character who speaks in it — a medium close-up in the
   scene's set: their pose, expression and what they hold, face toward the camera, lips closed, nobody
   else in frame. Make them in one MakeKeyframes call with "character_id" set on each frame, review and
   choose them the same way, with ChooseKeyframe {"scene_id", "character_id", "candidate"}.
5. ChooseKeyframe lists what is still needed; finish when nothing is.

Reply with each scene's chosen frames and review scores, and any concerns.
""",
            "llm_config": ${routine_llm},
            "tools": ["ReadCast", "ReadScreenplay", "ReadKeyframes", "MakeReferenceSheets", "MakeKeyframes",
                      "ChooseKeyframe"],
            "max_execution_seconds": 3600
        },

        {
            "name": "ShotPlanner",
            "function": {
                "description": "Turns the screenplay into the render pipeline's shot plan on the coverage keyframes, and validates it.",
                "parameters": ${agent_parameters}
            },
            "instructions": """
Call ReadCast, ReadScreenplay and ReadKeyframes, then plan the shots, scene by scene, like a film editor
covering a dialogue scene. The video model makes whoever is on screen speak the line, so the person on
screen during a line must be the one saying it.

- Shot ids are unique across the film: a short prefix from the scene id plus a number, e.g. "st1",
  "st2" for "studio" and "ga1" for "gala".
- "start" is one of:
  "establishing" — from the scene's establishing frame; every scene opens this way, on a silent shot.
  "single"       — from the speaker's single in this scene (set "character").
  "continue"     — from the last frame of the previous shot, or of "from": an earlier shot in the scene.
                   Only when that shot shows the same character.
  "fresh"        — no start frame: a cutaway with no character in it ("character": null).
- On-camera line: "character" (the speaker), "line", and an action that ends with the delivery, e.g.
  "Andie lifts the palette and says proudly". Start it on the speaker's single, or continue a shot of the
  same speaker. When the speaker changes, cut to the new speaker's single. No "seconds".
- Off-camera line (narration, or a voice heard while we watch someone react): "off_camera": true,
  "speaker" (who says it), "character" (who is on screen, listening, or null), "seconds" 3-5.
- Silent shots: "seconds" between 3 and 5.
- "camera": a short note such as "Static camera." or "Slow push-in."
- Refer to props with placeholders after "the": "Andie opens the {palette}" for the prop with id "palette".
- For wide shots where the character is tiny, add "identity_check": false.

Call SavePlan with {"scenes": [{"id": ..., "shots": [...]}]}. Fix every error it reports and save again.
Reply with the number of shots and lines and the estimated length.
""",
            "tools": ["ReadCast", "ReadScreenplay", "ReadKeyframes", "SavePlan"],
            "max_execution_seconds": 900
        },

        {"name": "ReadProduction", "class": "tools.ReadProduction",
         "function": {"description": "Title, story and target length of the production.", "parameters": ${no_parameters}}},
        {"name": "DescribeFirstFrame", "class": "tools.DescribeFirstFrame",
         "function": {"description": "A precise visual description of the production's first frame (style, characters, setting, light, framing).", "parameters": ${no_parameters}}},
        {"name": "SaveCast", "class": "tools.SaveCast",
         "function": {"description": "Validates and saves the cast.", "parameters": {
             "type": "object",
             "properties": {"cast": {"type": "object", "description": "{style: str, characters: [{id, name, bible, voice_phrase, head_query}], props: {id: description}}"}},
             "required": ["cast"]}}},
        {"name": "ReadCast", "class": "tools.ReadCast",
         "function": {"description": "The saved cast.", "parameters": ${no_parameters}}},
        {"name": "SaveScreenplay", "class": "tools.SaveScreenplay",
         "function": {"description": "Validates and saves the screenplay.", "parameters": {
             "type": "object",
             "properties": {"screenplay": {"type": "object", "description": "{logline: str, scenes: [{id, setting, audio, opening, beats: [{action, character?, line?, delivery?}]}]}"}},
             "required": ["screenplay"]}}},
        {"name": "ReadScreenplay", "class": "tools.ReadScreenplay",
         "function": {"description": "The saved screenplay.", "parameters": ${no_parameters}}},
        {"name": "ReadVoices", "class": "tools.ReadVoices",
         "function": {"description": "The voices cast so far: per character, every candidate and the chosen one.", "parameters": ${no_parameters}}},
        {"name": "CastVoice", "class": "tools.CastVoice",
         "function": {"description": "Designs one character's voice from a description, speaking the reference text (about 10 s), and compares it with the voices already cast.", "parameters": {
             "type": "object",
             "properties": {"character_id": {"type": "string"},
                            "description": {"type": "string", "description": "The voice: gender, age, pitch, timbre, accent, pace, manner"},
                            "text": {"type": "string", "description": "15-30 words the character says in their usual manner"}},
             "required": ["character_id", "description", "text"]}}},
        {"name": "ReadKeyframes", "class": "tools.ReadKeyframes",
         "function": {"description": "Reference sheets and keyframe candidates made so far, per scene: the establishing frame and the singles.", "parameters": ${no_parameters}}},
        {"name": "MakeReferenceSheets", "class": "tools.MakeReferenceSheets",
         "function": {"description": "Makes every missing character turnaround sheet and prop sheet, in parallel (about a minute).", "parameters": ${no_parameters}}},
        {"name": "MakeKeyframes", "class": "tools.MakeKeyframes",
         "function": {"description": "Makes candidate keyframes in parallel and reviews each against the sheets (about 70 s for a batch). A frame without character_id is the scene's establishing frame; with character_id it is that character's single, in the set of the scene's chosen establishing frame.", "parameters": {
             "type": "object",
             "properties": {"frames": {"type": "array", "description": "[{scene_id, character_id?, description}]", "items": {
                 "type": "object",
                 "properties": {"scene_id": {"type": "string"}, "character_id": {"type": "string"}, "description": {"type": "string", "description": "The frame in detail"}},
                 "required": ["scene_id", "description"]}}},
             "required": ["frames"]}}},
        {"name": "ChooseKeyframe", "class": "tools.ChooseKeyframe",
         "function": {"description": "Locks a candidate as the scene's establishing frame, or (with character_id) as that character's single; lists the keyframes still needed.", "parameters": {
             "type": "object",
             "properties": {"scene_id": {"type": "string"}, "character_id": {"type": "string"}, "candidate": {"type": "integer"}},
             "required": ["scene_id", "candidate"]}}},
        {"name": "SavePlan", "class": "tools.SavePlan",
         "function": {"description": "Builds and validates the shot plan exactly as the renderer will read it, including the rule that the person on screen says the line, then saves it.", "parameters": {
             "type": "object",
             "properties": {"scenes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object"}, "description": "[{id, shots: [{id, start, from?, character?, speaker?, off_camera?, action, line?, camera?, seconds?, identity_check?}]}]"}},
             "required": ["scenes"]}}}
    ]
}
